    <string name="wifi_check_readout">Band: %1$s\nSignal: %2$s (%3$s)\nLink speed: %4$s</string>
    <string name="wifi_band_24">2.4 GHz</string>
    <string name="wifi_band_5">5 GHz</string>
    <string name="wifi_band_6">6 GHz</string>
    <string name="wifi_band_unknown">unknown</string>
    <string name="wifi_signal_excellent">excellent</string>
    <string name="wifi_signal_good">good</string>
    <string name="wifi_signal_fair">fair</string>
    <string name="wifi_signal_weak">weak</string>
    <string name="wifi_signal_very_weak">very weak</string>
    <string name="wifi_advice_24ghz">• You\'re on 2.4 GHz. It reaches further but is slower and more crowded. If your router has a 5 GHz network, connect the TV to that.</string>
    <string name="wifi_advice_weak_signal">• The signal is weak. Moving the router and TV closer, or clearing what\'s between them, helps more than any setting.</string>
    <string name="wifi_advice_slow_link">• The link speed is low for mirroring. Turn on Light stream and Lower resolution above.</string>
    <string name="wifi_advice_ethernet">• The biggest fix of all: a cheap Ethernet adapter for this TV takes its half of the Wi-Fi out of the picture.</string>
    <string name="wifi_advice_smooth">• Turn on Smooth playback above to ride out the drops that remain.</string>
    <string name="wifi_advice_good">• This connection looks healthy. If mirroring still stutters, the weak side is probably your iPhone or iPad\'s Wi-Fi — try it closer to the router.</string>
